# modules/dast/passive/checks-transport.rules - the §7.4 plaintext-exposure and
# mixed-content script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5;
# docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md DAST-30).  Owned by
# modules/dast/passive/transport.sh; ids are all `DAST-TRANSPORT-*`.  A tier-5
# ticket whose checks run at tier `passive`, which is why it lives here.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, WHICH IS WHAT rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9'S
# `checks-<name>.rules` ROW EXISTS FOR.  This directory's registry used to be a
# single co-owned `checks.rules` that every tier-2 passive ticket appended to,
# because the §9 path table reserved only that one basename and any other name
# was `E070` - measured, on `modules/dast/passive/cookies.rules`, by
# `tests/lint-rules.sh` itself.  DAST-05..DAST-11 are peers with no ordering
# between them, so any two built in parallel produced an add/add conflict on that
# one file, and the mitigation ("take both sides") was a convention that has to be
# re-taught to every author and is silently wrong exactly once before anyone
# notices.  §9 now also reserves the `checks-` PREFIX, so each ticket owns its own
# file and there is no shared file left to conflict on.
#
# Nothing about identity moved with these records.  §9.5.1's owning-module map
# keys on the DIRECTORY, so E018/E081 still hold every id here to the `DAST-`
# prefix; a check id is unchanged by the file it lives in, so E019 uniqueness is
# still repository-wide; and `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh) globs every
# `*.rules` under `modules/dast/` at any depth with no per-file allowlist, so this
# file loads alongside its siblings with no registration step.  The records below
# were moved BYTE-IDENTICALLY out of the shared file: a check id feeds the finding
# fingerprint (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 5), so a split is a move and never an
# edit.
#
#
# THE SCOPE BOUNDARY, STATED HERE SO NEITHER PACK CAN REPORT THE SAME DEFECT
# TWICE.  Three checks in this directory neighbour these five and each owns a
# piece the DAST-TRANSPORT-* family must not claim:
#
#   passive/tls.sh (DAST-07) owns the CONNECTION - the handshake, the
#     certificate chain and its dates and hostname binding, the negotiated
#     protocol version and cipher suite.  Anything about whether the encryption
#     is any good is a DAST-TLS-* finding and never one of these.  These five
#     never inspect a certificate and never open a TLS connection of their own;
#     they read what came back through `http_request` like every other passive
#     check.  The two families are complements, which is what §7.4's own
#     sentence for this bullet says, and the division is: tls.sh asks "is this
#     encryption trustworthy", transport.sh asks "was it used at all".
#
#   passive/headers.sh (DAST-05) owns Strict-Transport-Security, all three of
#     DAST-HDR-HSTS_MISSING-01, DAST-HDR-HSTS_WEAK-01 and
#     DAST-HDR-HSTS_MALFORMED-01.  "This HTTPS response does not pin the
#     browser to HTTPS" is that family.  DAST-TRANSPORT-NO_HTTPS_REDIRECT-01 is
#     deliberately NOT that finding and does not restate it: HSTS is a promise
#     made ON the secure response about FUTURE requests, and the redirect check
#     is about what the plaintext origin does with the request in front of it.
#     A target can have a perfect HSTS header and still serve port 80, and a
#     target can redirect correctly and send no HSTS; the two findings have
#     different fixes and both remediations name the other as the companion
#     control rather than duplicating it.
#
#   passive/cookies.sh (DAST-06) owns the Secure ATTRIBUTE
#     (DAST-COOKIE-NO_SECURE-01): whether a browser would send this cookie over
#     plaintext in future.  DAST-TRANSPORT-PLAINTEXT_SENSITIVE-01 records that
#     the server ALREADY sent one over plaintext, which no attribute can undo,
#     and its evidence names DAST-COOKIE-NO_SECURE-01 explicitly as the separate
#     subject rather than re-reporting it.
#
# FIVE IDS RATHER THAN ONE, because the DAST location profile
# (target, method, path_template, param_location, param_name) carries NO
# component naming the defect - so a single DAST-TRANSPORT-EXPOSURE-01 would
# make a plaintext login page and a mixed script reference on the same path
# collide on one fingerprint and dedupe to one finding.  The active/passive
# mixed-content split is not a severity opinion either: browsers BLOCK
# blockable mixed content and merely downgrade the padlock for the
# optionally-blockable kind, so they are different facts about what a real
# visitor experiences.
#
# The type tag is `passive`, matching the phase table's own
# `passive/transport.sh:passive` floor (modules/dast/engine.sh, whose note there
# records why this ticket moved that row out of the tier-5 block).  Nothing here
# changes target state - every request is a plain GET to a URL the operator
# configured or an earlier phase already fetched - so the passive tier is the
# whole gate and `intrusive` is deliberately not set on any of them.

id: DAST-TRANSPORT-PLAINTEXT_SENSITIVE-01
title: Sensitive content is served over plaintext HTTP
script: passive/transport.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Serve this content over HTTPS only. Redirect the plaintext origin
  to `https://` with a 301 before any body is written, then add
  `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` so a browser
  that has visited once never attempts plaintext again. A redirect alone does
  not undo this exposure: the first request, and anything it carried, was
  already on the wire in the clear, so any credential or session token observed
  here must be treated as compromised and rotated.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TRANSPORT-NO_HTTPS_REDIRECT-01
title: Plaintext HTTP endpoint does not redirect to HTTPS
script: passive/transport.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Answer the plaintext origin with a 301 to the identical `https://`
  URL and nothing else - no body, no cookie, no content negotiation. Serving
  real content on port 80 means every visitor who types the bare hostname is
  served over an unauthenticated channel that any network position can rewrite.
  Follow the redirect with an HSTS header on the HTTPS response so the second
  visit never touches port 80 at all; that header is
  DAST-HDR-HSTS_MISSING-01's subject, and the two controls are companions
  rather than substitutes.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TRANSPORT-MIXED_ACTIVE-01
title: HTTPS document loads active sub-resources over plaintext HTTP
script: passive/transport.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Change these references to `https://`, or to a protocol-relative
  or root-relative form, both of which inherit the document scheme. Active
  mixed content - script, stylesheet, iframe, object, embed - executes inside
  the origin, so a network attacker who rewrites one of them owns the page
  regardless of how good the TLS on the document itself is. Every current
  browser blocks these outright, so the affected feature is already broken for
  real users, not merely insecure. A
  `Content-Security-Policy: upgrade-insecure-requests` header is a migration
  aid, not a fix, because it protects only browsers that honour it.
references: CWE-311
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TRANSPORT-MIXED_PASSIVE-01
title: HTTPS document loads passive sub-resources over plaintext HTTP
script: passive/transport.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Change these references to `https://` or to a scheme-relative
  form. Passive mixed content - images, audio, video, track files - is still
  loaded by browsers rather than blocked, which is why it is reported
  separately and at a lower severity than the active kind: nothing executes,
  but the resource is unauthenticated so its bytes can be replaced in transit,
  the request leaks the referring page to any observer, and the browser
  withholds the secure padlock from the whole document.
references: CWE-311
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

id: DAST-TRANSPORT-MIXED_FORM-01
title: HTTPS document submits a form to a plaintext HTTP action
script: passive/transport.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-319
owasp: A02:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Point the form action at the `https://` URL. A secure page
  carrying an insecure form action is worse than an insecure page: the padlock
  tells the user their input is protected while the submission itself - every
  field, including any password or payment detail - is sent in the clear and
  can be read or altered by any network position. Browsers show a separate
  interstitial for exactly this reason. Fix the action rather than relying on a
  server-side redirect, which happens only after the plaintext request has
  already been sent.
references: CWE-319
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/
